  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980056/assets/80fca5dc871cd083e638472abaf26b2a8ba91e81cfa5372df7398b4b63346dfa.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=5$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=10$",
  "worked_solution": "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking a pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=5$ without taking a pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the function is not continuous at $x=10$, it can't be differentiable at that point.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true.",
